  { q: "Is the Legend of the White Snake a true historical story?", a: "No, it is a folktale and legend, not a confirmed historical event. While it is set in real locations like Hangzhou and Mount Emei, the characters and magical events are fictional, evolving through centuries of oral and literary traditions [8]." },
  { q: "Why is the snake spirit considered a good character in Chinese culture?", a: "In this specific legend, the white snake spirit, Bai Suzhen, is portrayed as compassionate, loyal, and devoted to healing the sick. This subverts the common Western association of snakes with deceit, showing how animal symbolism can vary greatly across different cultural storytelling traditions [6]." },
  { q: "Who is the villain in the Legend of the White Snake?", a: "The Buddhist monk Fahai is typically viewed as the antagonist because he separates the loving couple. However, in older versions of the tale, he was sometimes depicted as a righteous figure trying to maintain the boundary between the human and spirit worlds [6]." },
  { q: "How has the White Snake story changed over time?", a: "The story has evolved significantly from its early origins in the Tang Dynasty to modern times. While early versions sometimes focused more on the dangers of spirits, later adaptations, including modern films and animations, heavily emphasize the romantic and tragic elements of the forbidden love story [7]." }
];
