  <h1>What Is the Legend of the White Snake?</h1>
  <div class="zh">白蛇传</div>
  <p>The Legend of the White Snake (白蛇传, Bái Shé Zhuàn) is one of China's most famous folktales, centering on a forbidden romance between a human scholar, Xu Xian, and a female snake spirit, Bai Suzhen. After centuries of spiritual cultivation, she takes human form to experience love. However, their marriage faces fierce opposition from a Buddhist monk who believes humans and spirits cannot mix, leading to a tragic and magical conflict.</p>
  <p style="color:#6a6153;font-size:13px;margin-top:10px">By <a href="/editorial-policy" style="color:inherit">yaopulife Editorial</a> · Published 2026-07-21 · Updated 2026-07-22</p>
</div></header>

<main class="wrap">
<section class="sec">
  <h2>The Core Tale of Forbidden Love</h2>
  <p>The Legend of the White Snake (白蛇传, Bái Shé Zhuàn, literally 'Tale of the White Snake') is a foundational Chinese folktale. In one common version, a white snake and a green snake engage in ascetic practices for centuries to gain magical powers and take human form [8].</p>
  <p>The white snake, named Bai Suzhen, meets and falls in love with a human scholar named Xu Xian. They marry and open a medicine shop, using her magical knowledge to heal the sick. However, their peaceful life is disrupted when a Buddhist monk named Fahai discovers her true identity and attempts to separate them, arguing that humans and spirits must not mix [6].</p>
</section>
<section class="sec">
  <h2>Key Characters and Their Symbolism</h2>
  <p><b>Bai Suzhen (白素贞, Bái Sùzhēn)</b> is the titular white snake spirit. She is usually described as a highly cultivated spirit who is deeply compassionate, loyal, and fiercely protective of her family [8]. Unlike many Western depictions of snakes, she represents pure devotion and self-sacrifice.</p>
  <p><b>Xu Xian (许仙, Xǔ Xiān)</b> is the mortal husband. He is often depicted as kind but easily swayed, representing the vulnerability of humans when faced with the supernatural. <b>Fahai (法海, Fǎhǎi)</b> is the monk who acts as the antagonist. While modern audiences often view him as a cruel villain breaking up a loving family, in older traditional contexts, he was sometimes seen as a righteous figure upholding the cosmic order [6].</p>
</section>
<section class="sec">
  <h2>Western Comparisons and Snake Symbolism</h2>
  <p>For Western readers, the Legend of the White Snake can be understood through familiar literary lenses. The central theme of a human falling in love with a non-human being who hides their true nature strongly echoes *Beauty and the Beast*. Both stories explore the idea that true humanity and love are defined by one's actions and heart, not their outward form or origins.</p>
  <p>Additionally, the intense opposition the couple faces from an authority figure mirrors the feuding families in *Romeo and Juliet*. It is important to note a major cultural difference in animal symbolism: while snakes in Western traditions are often associated with deceit or evil, Bai Suzhen subverts this by being the moral and emotional center of the story [2].</p>
</section>
<section class="sec">
  <h2>Evolution from Folktale to Modern Media</h2>
  <p>Because it originated as an oral tradition, there is no single 'original' text of the White Snake legend. The ancient legend dates back to the Tang Dynasty, and the earliest written versions appeared in later story collections [7]. The story has evolved over centuries, appearing in Ming dynasty literature, traditional Peking opera, and regional storytelling.</p>
  <p>Today, the legend remains highly relevant in pop culture. It has been adapted into countless television dramas, films, and modern animations, proving its status as a timeless story of forbidden love that continues to resonate with new generations [7].</p>
</section>

